Here’s the **ZeroAI framework** I’d lock in as the canonical architecture.

## ZeroAI

**ZeroAI is not an AI model.**  
It is the deterministic operating system around AI models.

The model reasons.  
ZeroAI controls **state, execution, permissions, memory, evidence, and certification**.

### Core architecture

| Plane | Responsibility | Typical implementation |
|---|---|---|
| **1. Control Plane** | Decides what runs, in what order, retries, dependencies, permissions, blocking gates | TypeScript, JSON schemas, workflow engine |
| **2. Instruction Plane** | Defines how specialized agents reason and what standards they follow | Markdown + typed schemas |
| **3. Execution Plane** | Executes tools, APIs, code, browser actions, database operations and agent jobs | Next.js/Node, workers, MCP/tools |
| **4. Memory Plane** | Maintains compact durable state instead of replaying entire chat/history | Postgres/Neon, event log, fact graph, ZeroMemory |
| **5. Observation Plane** | Records evidence of what actually happened | telemetry, logs, traces, screenshots, test results, hashes |

The key rule is:

> **Prompting is instruction. Orchestration is architecture.**

A folder full of `architect.md`, `qa.md`, `security.md`, etc. is not a multi-agent system until something deterministic controls when those agents execute, what they are allowed to do, and whether their output is accepted.

