## Event sourcing

Everything important becomes an immutable event.

```ts
type ZeroEvent = {
  eventId: string
  workspaceId: string
  actor: string
  action: string
  timestamp: string
  inputHash: string
  outputHash?: string
  parentHash: string
  evidenceRefs: string[]
}
```

That gives you:

```text
event 001
   ↓ hash
event 002
   ↓ hash
event 003
```

If old history changes, the chain breaks.

That's the foundation of the **tamper-evident ZeroLedger**.

---

# ZeroLedger

Every meaningful execution should leave an immutable audit record.

Not:

> Agent says tests passed.

Instead:

```text
QA_AGENT claimed: PASS
Test runner exit code: 0
Tests: 842 / 842 passed
Commit: abc123
Artifact hash: ...
Timestamp: ...
Environment: production candidate
Evidence: attached
```

The certification engine evaluates the evidence.

The agent does **not certify itself**.

---

# ZeroPolicy

Models should never decide their own authority.

Permissions belong in a deterministic broker.

```text
READ
WRITE
DELETE
DEPLOY
SEND_EMAIL
CHARGE_CARD
MERGE_PR
MODIFY_DATABASE
ROTATE_SECRET
```

Example:

```ts
{
  agent: "backend",
  action: "modify_database",
  environment: "production",
  policy: "approval-required"
}
```

The model can request authority.

It cannot manufacture authority.

---

# ZeroGate

Every important transition has a gate.

Example software release:

```text
CODE COMPLETE
     ↓
TYPECHECK
     ↓
UNIT TESTS
     ↓
INTEGRATION TESTS
     ↓
CRUD CERTIFICATION
     ↓
SECURITY
     ↓
MIGRATION SAFETY
     ↓
E2E
     ↓
PRODUCTION READINESS
     ↓
RELEASE
```

A failed gate blocks the next state.

Not because the AI thinks it should.

Because the state machine says it must.

---

# ZeroCert

Certification is evidence-driven.

Use evidence states such as:

```text
VERIFIED
PARTIAL
UNKNOWN
FAILED
```

Example:

```ts
{
  requirement: "Customer deletion works",
  state: "VERIFIED",
  evidence: {
    test: "customer-delete.spec.ts",
    executionId: "...",
    response: 204,
    databaseVerification: true
  }
}
```

No evidence = **UNKNOWN**, not success.

---

# ZeroState

Agents never own canonical state.

Canonical state lives outside inference.

```text
Database
Event ledger
Task graph
Artifacts
Evidence
Memory graph
Policies
```

AI receives a projection of that state.

It does not become the state.

This prevents the exact kind of agent drift you’ve been fighting.

